export const reportMd: string = "# NAND TO TETRIS CAPSTONE PROJECT REPORT\n## MAKE THE COMPUTER DRAW A BOX\n\n---\n\n### PAGE 1 \u2014 PROJECT OVERVIEW\n\n#### Course Information\n- **Course Name:** Nand to Tetris \u2013 I\n- **Course Code:** ETCNTC301 / ETCCNT301\n- **Institution:** School of Engineering & Technology, K.R. Mangalam University\n- **Assessment Scheme:** Capstone Project (10 Marks, Individual Work)\n- **Academic Semester:** Odd Semester 2026\u20132027\n\n#### Student Information\n- **Student Name:** Prithvi Saini\n- **University Roll Number:** 2501730041\n- **Personal Number K:** **41** (Calculated as the last two digits of University Roll Number `2501730041`)\n\n#### Personal Parameter Calculations\n| Parameter | Formula | Calculated Value |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Roll Number** | Given Roll Number | `2501730041` |\n| **Personal Number K** | Last two digits of Roll Number | **41** |\n| **K mod 3** | $41 \\pmod 3$ | **2** (Feature: Duplicate Label Detection) |\n| **K mod 4** | $41 \\pmod 4$ | **1** |\n| **Box Starting Row** | $K$ | **Row 41** |\n| **Box Height** | Fixed Specification | **20 screen rows** (Rows 41 to 60) |\n| **Box Width in Pixels** | $16 \\times ((K \\bmod 4) + 1)$ | $16 \\times (1 + 1) =$ **32 pixels** (Cols 0 to 31) |\n| **16-bit Words per Row** | $(K \\bmod 4) + 1$ | **2 words** |\n| **Starting Screen Address** | $\\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times K)$ | $16384 + (32 \\times 41) =$ **17696** |\n| **Ending Screen Row** | $K + 19$ | $41 + 19 =$ **Row 60** |\n| **Ending Screen Address** | $\\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times 60) + 1$ | $16384 + 1920 + 1 =$ **18305** |\n\n#### Project Objective\nThe objective of this Capstone Project is to design, construct, integrate, and verify a complete working computer system from fundamental logic gates up to an interactive assembly language application. The project encompasses three primary milestones:\n1. **Part A (Hardware Layer):** Building and verifying the Hack Computer platform in Hardware Description Language (HDL), integrating `CPU.hdl`, `Memory.hdl`, and `Computer.hdl`.\n2. **Part B (Software Toolchain Layer):** Developing an independent, fully-featured two-pass Hack Assembler in Python (`assembler.py`) supporting predefined symbols, label resolution, dynamic variable allocation starting at RAM[16], and a customized diagnostic feature ($K \\bmod 3 = 2$: Duplicate Label Detection).\n3. **Part C (Application Layer):** Authoring `Box.asm` in Hack Assembly Language to continuously monitor the keyboard register (`KBD`, RAM[24576]), dynamically drawing a 32\u00d720 black box starting at Row 41 while any key is pressed, and cleanly erasing it when released.\n\n#### The Hack Computer Architecture\nThe Hack computer is a 16-bit Von Neumann architecture consisting of a Central Processing Unit (CPU), an instruction memory (ROM32K), and a unified data memory space (Memory, 32K words) encompassing 16K of General RAM, 8K of Memory-Mapped Screen space (256 rows \u00d7 512 pixels), and a Memory-Mapped Keyboard input register.\n\n---\n\n### PAGE 2 \u2014 COMPUTER ARCHITECTURE AND ASSEMBLER\n\n#### 1. Hardware Architecture Implementation\n\n##### A. CPU.hdl\nThe Hack CPU integrates an Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU), two programmer-visible 16-bit registers (A-register and D-register), a 15-bit Program Counter (PC), and comprehensive decoding logic.\n- **Instruction Decoding:** Bit 15 determines the instruction class. A `0` indicates an A-instruction (`@value`), loading a 15-bit constant into the A-register. A `1` signifies a C-instruction (`111accccccdddjjj`), controlling the ALU operation (`c1..c6`), source selection (`a`), destinations (`d1..d3`), and jump conditions (`j1..j3`).\n- **ALU and Multiplexing:** The ALU computes operations between the D-register (x-input) and either the A-register or external memory `inM` (y-input, selected by bit 12).\n- **Control Flags and Program Counter:** Destination bits route results to `outM` (writeM if d3=1), `D-register` (load if d2=1), and `A-register` (load if d1=1 or A-instruction). Jump logic checks ALU zero (`zr`) and negative (`ng`) status against jump bits (`j1, j2, j3`), setting `PC.load=1` upon taken branch; otherwise, `PC.inc=1`. Reset asserts `PC=0`.\n\n##### B. Memory.hdl\n`Memory.hdl` manages the 32K address space through address decoding:\n- `address[14] == 0`: Routes reads/writes to `RAM16K` (0 to 16383 / `0x0000` to `0x3FFF`).\n- `address[14..13] == 10`: Routes reads/writes to `Screen` (16384 to 24575 / `0x4000` to `0x5FFF`).\n- `address[14..13] == 11`: Routes reads from the `Keyboard` register (24576 / `0x6000`). Keyboard is read-only; write attempts are safely sinked.\n- Output multiplexing is performed using `Mux4Way16` driven by `address[13..14]`.\n\n##### C. Computer.hdl\n`Computer.hdl` unifies the top-level system:\n- `ROM32K` addresses instructions via `CPU.pc` and delivers `instruction` to the CPU.\n- `CPU` reads `Memory.out` via `inM`, emits computed values via `outM`, drives memory write enables via `writeM`, and directs addresses via `addressM`.\n- System reset pin connects directly to `CPU.reset`, initiating execution from ROM[0].\n\n#### 2. Two-Pass Python Assembler (`assembler.py`)\n\nThe assembler converts Hack assembly programs into 16-bit binary machine code without external dependencies:\n- **First Pass (Label Resolution):** The assembler strips comments and whitespace while preserving original 1-based source line numbers. When a label `(LABEL)` is encountered, it is bound to the current ROM instruction address without advancing the counter.\n- **Second Pass (Code Generation & Variable Allocation):**\n  - **A-instructions (`@symbol`):** Constants (e.g., `@17696`) are directly converted to 15-bit binary with a leading `0`. Predefined symbols (`SCREEN`, `KBD`, `R0-R15`, `SP`, `LCL`, `ARG`, `THIS`, `THAT`) and labels are resolved to their mapped addresses. Unseen user symbols are dynamically allocated sequential RAM addresses starting at `RAM[16]`.\n  - **C-instructions (`dest=comp;jump`):** Mnemonic fields are parsed and encoded using the standard Hack binary translation tables, prefixed by `111`.\n\n#### 3. Personal K-Based Feature ($K \\bmod 3 = 2$: Duplicate Label Detection)\nBecause $K = 41$ and $41 \\pmod 3 = 2$, the assembler implements duplicate label detection:\n- During the first pass, every label declaration is registered alongside its source line number in a declaration map.\n- If a subsequent declaration of the same label occurs, the assembler issues an explicit warning specifying both line numbers:\n  `Warning: Duplicate label 'LOOP' found at line 13. First definition is at line 6.`\n- The assembler preserves the initial declaration address in the symbol table (avoiding silent overwrites) and continues safe assembly according to university specifications.\n\n---\n\n### PAGE 3 \u2014 BOX PROGRAM (`Box.asm`)\n\n#### 1. Geometric and Memory Calculations for $K = 41$\n- **Screen Geometry:** 256 rows \u00d7 512 pixels. Each row consists of 32 words (each word represents 16 horizontal pixels).\n- **Box Dimensions:**\n  - Starting row = $K = 41$.\n  - Height = 20 screen rows (Rows 41 to 60 inclusive).\n  - Width = $16 \\times ((K \\bmod 4) + 1) = 16 \\times (1 + 1) = 32$ pixels.\n  - Words per row = 2 words (Word 0 and Word 1 of each row).\n- **Memory Address Mapping:**\n  - Row 41 starts at: $\\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times 41) = 16384 + 1312 =$ **17696**.\n  - Row 60 starts at: $\\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times 60) = 16384 + 1920 = 18304$.\n  - Ending word of the box (Row 60, Word 1) = $18304 + 1 =$ **18305**.\n\n#### 2. Keyboard Input and Dynamic Display Algorithm\nThe program executes an infinite real-time polling loop:\n```text\n                  +-------------------------+\n                  |    Read RAM[KBD]        |\n                  |     (Addr 24576)        |\n                  +------------+------------+\n                               |\n                        [KBD == 0 ?]\n                        /          \\\n                   YES /            \\ NO (Key Pressed)\n                      v              v\n            +----------------+ +-----------------+\n            | Set color = 0  | | Set color = -1  |\n            | (White/Erase)  | | (Black/Draw)    |\n            +-------+--------+ +--------+--------+\n                    \\                  /\n                     \\                /\n                      v              v\n              +-------------------------------+\n              | row_addr = 17696 (Row 41)     |\n              | rows_left = 20                |\n              +---------------+---------------+\n                              |\n                     +--------v--------+\n            +------->| rows_left <= 0? |----+ YES (Finished Frame)\n            |        +--------+--------+    |\n            |              NO |             v\n            |                 v      +---------------+\n            |  RAM[row_addr]     = color     | Jump back to  |\n            |  RAM[row_addr + 1] = color     |  MAIN_LOOP    |\n            |  row_addr  = row_addr + 32     +---------------+\n            |  rows_left = rows_left - 1\n            +-----------------+\n```\n\n#### 3. Hack Assembly Implementation Highlights\n- **Keyboard Detection:**\n  ```assembly\n  (MAIN_LOOP)\n      @KBD\n      D=M\n      @KEY_PRESSED\n      D;JNE\n  (KEY_RELEASED)\n      @color\n      M=0\n      @DRAW_BOX\n      0;JMP\n  (KEY_PRESSED)\n      @color\n      M=-1\n  ```\n- **Row Drawing & Pointer Arithmetic:**\n  ```assembly\n  (ROW_LOOP)\n      @rows_left\n      D=M\n      @MAIN_LOOP\n      D;JLE\n      @color\n      D=M\n      @row_addr\n      A=M\n      M=D         // Word 0 (Cols 0..15)\n      @row_addr\n      A=M+1\n      M=D         // Word 1 (Cols 16..31)\n      @32\n      D=A\n      @row_addr\n      M=D+M       // Advance by 32 words to next row\n      @rows_left\n      M=M-1\n      @ROW_LOOP\n      0;JMP\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### PAGE 4 \u2014 TESTING, VERIFICATION AND LEARNING\n\n#### 1. HDL Verification Results\nAll top-level chips were simulated using the official Nand2Tetris Hardware Simulator toolchain. Results are verified against official reference comparison files:\n\n| Target Chip | Test Script | Status | Output Verification Result |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **CPU.hdl** | `CPU.tst` | **PASS** | `End of script - Comparison ended successfully` |\n| **Memory.hdl (RAM & Screen)** | `MemoryRAM.tst` | **PASS** | `End of script - Comparison ended successfully` |\n| **Memory.hdl (Interactive)** | `Memory.tst` | **PASS** | Passes interactive keyboard check ('K' and 'Y') in GUI |\n| **Computer.hdl (Add)** | `ComputerAdd.tst` | **PASS** | `End of script - Comparison ended successfully` |\n| **Computer.hdl (Max)** | `ComputerMax.tst` | **PASS** | `End of script - Comparison ended successfully` |\n| **Computer.hdl (Rect)** | `ComputerRect.tst` | **PASS** | `End of script - Comparison ended successfully` |\n\n*Built-in Chip Note:* Standard Project 5 primitive sequential components (`RAM16K`, `Screen`, `Keyboard`, `ROM32K`) were loaded via standard built-in simulator definitions, while `CPU.hdl`, `Memory.hdl`, and `Computer.hdl` represent complete custom HDL implementations.\n\n#### 2. Assembler Bit-for-Bit Verification Results\nOur Python assembler was benchmarked against the official Java-based Nand2Tetris assembler across the four benchmark programs:\n\n| Program Name | Generated Instructions | Official Instructions | Bit-for-Bit Match | Status |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **Add.asm** | 6 | 6 | **YES** | PASS (100% Match) |\n| **Max.asm** | 16 | 16 | **YES** | PASS (100% Match) |\n| **Rect.asm** | 25 | 25 | **YES** | PASS (100% Match) |\n| **Pong.asm** | 27,483 | 27,483 | **YES** | PASS (100% Match) |\n\n#### 3. Box Program Execution Evidence\n- **Automated CPU Emulator Test:** `BoxTest.tst` executed on `Box.hack` in the official Nand2Tetris CPU Emulator:\n  - While `KBD = 0`: `RAM[17696]` through `RAM[18305]` are `0`.\n  - While `KBD = 75` (Key 'K'): `RAM[17696]` through `RAM[18305]` transition to `-1` (black).\n  - While `KBD = 0` (Key released): Words immediately restore to `0` (white).\n  - Out-of-bounds boundary words (`RAM[17695]`, `RAM[17698]`, `RAM[18306]`) remain strictly `0`.\n  - CPU Emulator comparison reported: `End of script - Comparison ended successfully`.\n- **Rendered Visual Evidence:**\n  - `evidence/box/box_key_pressed.png`: Demonstrates 32\u00d720 box displayed at row 41.\n  - `evidence/box/box_key_released.png`: Demonstrates complete screen blanking upon release.\n  - `evidence/box/box_zoomed_detail.png`: Shows pixel-accurate 16\u00d7 zoom confirming column boundaries [0..31] and row boundaries [41..60].\n\n#### 4. Real Programming Mistake Encountered & Diagnosed\n- **Mistake:** During initial drafting of `Box.asm`, row pointer addition was written as:\n  ```assembly\n  @32\n  D=A\n  @row_addr\n  M=M+D\n  ```\n- **Symptom & Diagnosis:** When compiling with `assembler.py`, the assembler stopped with a clear diagnostic error:\n  `Assembly FAILED: Line 103: Invalid computation mnemonic 'M+D'`\n- **Root Cause Analysis:** In the Hack CPU architecture, the ALU hardware accepts `x = D` and `y = A` (or `y = M`). The official ALU computation table explicitly specifies `D+M` (x + y), but does NOT support `M+D` as a valid mnemonic because the hardware multiplexer is asymmetric.\n- **Correction:** The instruction was corrected to `M=D+M`. The assembler immediately accepted the line and generated the correct machine instruction (`1111000010001000`).\n\n#### 5. Conclusion\nThis Capstone Project successfully verified the complete computing stack: from hardware chip design and timing to compiler/assembler construction and machine-level graphics programming. All university acceptance criteria, personal roll number calculations ($K = 41$), and automated test suites passed with 100% accuracy.\n";

export const readmeMd: string = "# NAND TO TETRIS CAPSTONE PROJECT: MAKE THE COMPUTER DRAW A BOX\n**Course:** Nand to Tetris \u2013 I (ETCNTC301 / ETCCNT301)  \n**University:** School of Engineering & Technology, K.R. Mangalam University  \n**Student Name:** Prithvi Saini  \n**University Roll Number:** 2501730041  \n**Personal Parameter K:** 41  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Project Overview\n\nThis repository contains the complete, tested, and demonstrative implementation of the Nand to Tetris Capstone Project. The project builds a complete computing stack in three integrated milestones:\n1. **Hardware Implementation (HDL):** Working implementations of `CPU.hdl`, `Memory.hdl`, and `Computer.hdl`.\n2. **Software Toolchain (Python):** An independent two-pass Hack Assembler (`assembler.py`) supporting predefined symbols, label resolution, sequential RAM variable allocation from address 16, and an automated K-dependent feature ($K \\bmod 3 = 2$: Duplicate Label Detection).\n3. **Application Program (Hack Assembly):** `Box.asm`, an interactive Hack assembly program that continuously monitors keyboard input (`KBD = 24576`) to draw a 32\u00d720 black box starting at Row 41 while a key is held, and erases it when released.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Directory Structure\n\n```text\ncapstone/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 HDL/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 CPU.hdl                     # Complete Hack Central Processing Unit\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Memory.hdl                  # 32K memory space (RAM16K + Screen + Keyboard)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Computer.hdl                # Top-level Hack Computer integration\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 CPU.tst, CPU.cmp            # Official CPU test script & comparison\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Memory.tst, Memory.cmp      # Official interactive Memory test script\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 MemoryRAM.tst, MemoryRAM.cmp# Automated Memory RAM/Screen test script\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 ComputerAdd.tst, .cmp       # Computer integration test with Add.hack\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 ComputerMax.tst, .cmp       # Computer integration test with Max.hack\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ComputerRect.tst, .cmp      # Computer integration test with Rect.hack\n\u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 assembler.py                    # Independent Python two-pass Hack Assembler\n\u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Box.asm                         # Hack Assembly program for the interactive box\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Box.hack                        # 16-bit binary machine code assembled by assembler.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 BoxTest.tst                     # Automated CPU Emulator test script for Box.hack\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 BoxTest.cmp                     # Expected comparison output for Box.hack\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 BoxTest.out                     # Actual CPU Emulator execution log\n\u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 test_programs/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Add.asm, Add.hack           # Benchmark test 1 (6 instructions)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Max.asm, Max.hack           # Benchmark test 2 (16 instructions)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Rect.asm, Rect.hack         # Benchmark test 3 (25 instructions)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Pong.asm, Pong.hack         # Benchmark test 4 (27,483 instructions)\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 DuplicateLabelTest.asm      # Verification program for K mod 3 = 2 feature\n\u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 evidence/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 hdl/                        # HDL simulation logs & comparison outputs\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 assembler/                  # Assembler bit-for-bit comparison reports\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 box/                        # Visual screenshots and CPU Emulator logs\n\u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 PROJECT_REPORT.md               # 4-page formal project report for ZOSIMA\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 VIVA_PREPARATION.md             # Complete 5-minute viva question & answer guide\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md                       # Comprehensive setup and usage instructions\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Personal Parameter Calculations ($K = 41$)\n\n- **University Roll Number:** `2501730041`\n- **$K$ (last two digits):** `41`\n- **$K \\bmod 3$:** $41 \\bmod 3 =$ **`2`** $\\rightarrow$ **Feature:** Duplicate Label Detection with line numbers and warning\n- **$K \\bmod 4$:** $41 \\bmod 4 =$ **`1`**\n- **Box Starting Row:** $K =$ **`Row 41`**\n- **Box Height:** **`20 screen rows`** (Row 41 through Row 60)\n- **Box Width in Pixels:** $16 \\times ((K \\bmod 4) + 1) = 16 \\times (1 + 1) =$ **`32 pixels`** (Columns 0 to 31)\n- **16-bit Words per Row:** $(K \\bmod 4) + 1 =$ **`2 words`**\n- **Starting Screen Memory Address:** $\\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times K) = 16384 + (32 \\times 41) =$ **`17696`**\n- **Ending Screen Row:** $K + 19 = 41 + 19 =$ **`Row 60`**\n- **Ending Screen Memory Address:** $\\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times 60) + 1 = 16384 + 1920 + 1 =$ **`18305`**\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to Test HDL Chips\n\nYou can test the HDL chips using the Nand2Tetris Hardware Simulator:\n\n### Interactive Testing (GUI Mode)\n1. Open the Hardware Simulator:\n   ```cmd\n   T:\\nandtotertris\\official_nand2tetris\\tools\\HardwareSimulator.bat\n   ```\n2. Click **File -> Load Script** and navigate to `capstone/HDL/CPU.tst` (or `Memory.tst`, `ComputerAdd.tst`).\n3. Set the animation speed slider to fast and click **Run**.\n4. When running `Memory.tst`, hold down the 'K' and 'Y' keys when prompted to verify the Keyboard register.\n\n### Automated Testing (Command Line)\nTo execute tests directly in batch mode and verify comparison results:\n```powershell\ncd T:\\nandtotertris\\official_nand2tetris\\tools\njava -cp \"C:\\Users\\saini\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\0607bb33-baa7-4f87-9b70-7c69cf082c72\\scratch;.;bin\\classes;bin\\lib\\*\" RunHW \"T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\\HDL\\CPU.tst\"\njava -cp \"C:\\Users\\saini\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\0607bb33-baa7-4f87-9b70-7c69cf082c72\\scratch;.;bin\\classes;bin\\lib\\*\" RunHW \"T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\\HDL\\MemoryRAM.tst\"\njava -cp \"C:\\Users\\saini\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\0607bb33-baa7-4f87-9b70-7c69cf082c72\\scratch;.;bin\\classes;bin\\lib\\*\" RunHW \"T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\\HDL\\ComputerAdd.tst\"\njava -cp \"C:\\Users\\saini\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\0607bb33-baa7-4f87-9b70-7c69cf082c72\\scratch;.;bin\\classes;bin\\lib\\*\" RunHW \"T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\\HDL\\ComputerMax.tst\"\njava -cp \"C:\\Users\\saini\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\0607bb33-baa7-4f87-9b70-7c69cf082c72\\scratch;.;bin\\classes;bin\\lib\\*\" RunHW \"T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\\HDL\\ComputerRect.tst\"\n```\n**Expected Output:** Each test displays `End of script - Comparison ended successfully`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. How to Run the Python Assembler\n\nThe assembler is an independent Python script requiring no external libraries (pure standard Python 3):\n\n```powershell\n# Basic usage (outputs .hack next to source file)\npython capstone/assembler.py capstone/Box.asm\n\n# Explicit output file\npython capstone/assembler.py capstone/test_programs/Add.asm capstone/test_programs/Add.hack\n\n# Test the K mod 3 = 2 Duplicate Label Detection feature:\npython capstone/assembler.py capstone/test_programs/DuplicateLabelTest.asm\n```\n\n**Terminal Output Example:**\n```text\n==================================================\n Hack Two-Pass Assembler\n Student Roll No: 2501730041 | K = 41 (K mod 3 = 2)\n Duplicate Label Detection Enabled\n==================================================\nInput file: capstone/test_programs/DuplicateLabelTest.asm\nWarning: Duplicate label 'LOOP' found at line 13. First definition is at line 6.\nWarning: Duplicate label 'END' found at line 21. First definition is at line 18.\n--------------------------------------------------\nSource lines:          23\nMachine instructions:  16\nUnique labels:         2\nDuplicate warnings:    2\nVariables allocated:   2\nOutput saved to:       capstone/test_programs/DuplicateLabelTest.hack\nStatus:                SUCCESS\n==================================================\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. How to Run and Test `Box.asm`\n\n### Step 1: Assemble `Box.asm` into `Box.hack`\n```powershell\ncd T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\npython assembler.py Box.asm Box.hack\n```\n\n### Step 2: Run in CPU Emulator (GUI)\n1. Open the CPU Emulator:\n   ```cmd\n   T:\\nandtotertris\\official_nand2tetris\\tools\\CPUEmulator.bat\n   ```\n2. Click **File -> Load Program** and open `capstone/Box.hack`.\n3. Set **Animate** to \"No animation\" and speed to Fast.\n4. Click the **Run** button.\n5. Click on the **Keyboard** icon in the emulator:\n   - Hold down any keyboard key: A 32\u00d720 black rectangular box appears at row 41 on the left edge.\n   - Release the key: The box immediately disappears.\n\n### Step 3: Run the Automated Verification Script\nRun the automated test script that verifies screen memory addresses and keyboard reactions:\n```powershell\ncd T:\\nandtotertris\\official_nand2tetris\\tools\njava -cp \"C:\\Users\\saini\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\0607bb33-baa7-4f87-9b70-7c69cf082c72\\scratch;.;bin\\classes;bin\\lib\\*\" RunCPU \"T:\\nandtotertris\\capstone\\BoxTest.tst\"\n```\n**Result:** Displays `End of script - Comparison ended successfully`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Verification Summary\n\n| Component | Test Case | Target / Reference | Result | Status |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: |\n| **CPU** | `CPU.tst` | `CPU.cmp` | Exact match | **PASS** |\n| **Memory** | `MemoryRAM.tst` | `MemoryRAM.cmp` | Exact match | **PASS** |\n| **Computer** | `ComputerAdd.tst` | `ComputerAdd.cmp` | Exact match | **PASS** |\n| **Computer** | `ComputerMax.tst` | `ComputerMax.cmp` | Exact match | **PASS** |\n| **Computer** | `ComputerRect.tst` | `ComputerRect.cmp` | Exact match | **PASS** |\n| **Assembler** | `Add.asm` (6 instrs) | Official `Add.hack` | Bit-for-bit match | **PASS** |\n| **Assembler** | `Max.asm` (16 instrs) | Official `Max.hack` | Bit-for-bit match | **PASS** |\n| **Assembler** | `Rect.asm` (25 instrs) | Official `Rect.hack` | Bit-for-bit match | **PASS** |\n| **Assembler** | `Pong.asm` (27,483 instrs)| Official `Pong.hack` | Bit-for-bit match | **PASS** |\n| **Feature** | `DuplicateLabelTest.asm` | Line warnings | Warning emitted, safe | **PASS** |\n| **Box Program** | `BoxTest.tst` | `BoxTest.cmp` | Exact memory match | **PASS** |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Required Software & Tools\n\n- **Java Runtime Environment (JRE/JDK):** Java 11 or higher (OpenJDK / Oracle JDK) for running the Nand2Tetris Hardware Simulator and CPU Emulator.\n- **Python:** Python 3.8 or higher for running `assembler.py`.\n- **Operating System:** Windows, macOS, or Linux.\n";

export const vivaMd: string = "# NAND TO TETRIS CAPSTONE \u2014 VIVA PREPARATION GUIDE\n**Student Name:** Prithvi Saini  \n**Roll Number:** 2501730041  \n**Personal Parameter K:** 41 ($K \\bmod 3 = 2$, $K \\bmod 4 = 1$)  \n**Course:** ETCNTC301 / ETCCNT301 \u2014 Nand to Tetris \u2013 I  \n**University:** School of Engineering & Technology, K.R. Mangalam University  \n\n---\n\n## SECTION A \u2014 HACK COMPUTER ARCHITECTURE\n\n### 1. What is a Hack computer?\n**Answer:** The Hack computer is a 16-bit Von Neumann computer architecture designed in the Nand to Tetris course. It contains a CPU, an instruction memory (`ROM32K`), and a unified data memory (`Memory`) mapped to 16K of RAM, 8K of display Screen, and 1 Keyboard input register. It executes two types of 16-bit instructions: A-instructions (addressing) and C-instructions (computation and branching).\n\n### 2. What is the function of `CPU.hdl`?\n**Answer:** `CPU.hdl` is the central execution unit. It fetches a 16-bit instruction from ROM, decodes whether it is an A-instruction or C-instruction, directs data between registers and the ALU, performs arithmetic/logic operations, computes condition flags (`zr` and `ng`), writes results to Memory when required, and determines the next instruction address for the Program Counter (`PC`).\n\n### 3. What is the function of `Memory.hdl`?\n**Answer:** `Memory.hdl` implements the complete 32K data address space using address decoding:\n- `0` to `16383` (`0x0000` to `0x3FFF`): General purpose Data RAM (`RAM16K`).\n- `16384` to `24575` (`0x4000` to `0x5FFF`): Memory-mapped display buffer (`Screen`).\n- `24576` (`0x6000`): Memory-mapped keyboard register (`Keyboard`).\nIt ensures write operations only alter the addressed device (and never write to the read-only Keyboard), while reading is multiplexed using address bits `address[13..14]`.\n\n### 4. Why do we need `Computer.hdl`?\n**Answer:** `Computer.hdl` is the top-level chip that interconnects the CPU, ROM32K, and Memory into a functional system. It routes the CPU's `pc` to the ROM address bus, feeds the fetched `instruction` into the CPU, and connects data lines (`outM`, `inM`, `addressM`, `writeM`) between CPU and Memory. It also exposes the master `reset` input.\n\n### 5. What is the role of the ALU?\n**Answer:** The Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) performs 16-bit arithmetic (addition, increment, negation) and logical (AND, OR, NOT) operations on two inputs: $x$ (from the D-register) and $y$ (from either the A-register or external memory `inM`). It is controlled by 6 control bits (`zx, nx, zy, ny, f, no`) and emits two status flags: `zr` (1 if output is zero) and `ng` (1 if output is negative).\n\n### 6. What is the role of the A and D registers?\n**Answer:** \n- **D-Register (Data Register):** Stores intermediate data values exclusively for ALU operations.\n- **A-Register (Address/Data Register):** Dual-purpose register. It can store a 15-bit constant, an address pointer to access RAM/Screen (`inM = RAM[A]`), or a jump target address for the Program Counter (`PC`).\n\n### 7. What does the Program Counter (PC) do?\n**Answer:** The Program Counter keeps track of the ROM address of the next instruction to fetch and execute. In each cycle:\n- If `reset == 1`: PC is set to 0.\n- Else if `load == 1` (jump condition met): PC loads the address stored in the A-register.\n- Else (`inc == 1`): PC increments by 1 for normal sequential execution.\n\n### 8. How do jump instructions work?\n**Answer:** C-instructions have 3 jump bits (`j1, j2, j3`). The CPU compares these bits against the ALU output flags:\n- `j1` checks if output is negative (`ng == 1`).\n- `j2` checks if output is zero (`zr == 1`).\n- `j3` checks if output is positive (`!zr && !ng == 1`).\nIf the condition evaluates to true on a C-instruction, `loadPC` is asserted, causing `PC` to load the value currently in register `A`.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION B \u2014 PYTHON ASSEMBLER (`assembler.py`)\n\n### 1. What is an assembler?\n**Answer:** An assembler is a software translator that converts human-readable symbolic assembly code (`.asm`) into binary machine language code (`.hack`) that can be executed directly by hardware logic.\n\n### 2. What is machine code?\n**Answer:** Machine code is a sequence of binary instructions composed purely of `0`s and `1`s. In Hack, each machine instruction is exactly 16 bits wide.\n\n### 3. Why do we use two passes?\n**Answer:** We need two passes to resolve **forward references** (jumping forward to a label defined later in the code). In a single pass, when the assembler sees `@LOOP` before `(LOOP)` has appeared, it does not know what ROM address `LOOP` will have. The first pass records all label addresses; the second pass can then translate all instructions knowing every target address.\n\n### 4. What happens during the first pass?\n**Answer:** \n1. Strips comments (`//...`) and whitespace, ignoring blank lines.\n2. Tracks original source line numbers for diagnostic reporting.\n3. Keeps a ROM instruction counter starting at 0.\n4. When encountering a label like `(LOOP)`, it maps `LOOP -> current ROM address` in the symbol table.\n5. Checks for duplicate label declarations ($K \\bmod 3 = 2$ feature).\n6. Labels do not advance the ROM counter.\n\n### 5. How are labels resolved?\n**Answer:** A label `(MY_LABEL)` points to the ROM address of the very next actual instruction. In the second pass, any A-instruction referring to `@MY_LABEL` is replaced by `@ROM_ADDR`, which is encoded as a 15-bit binary number with a leading 0.\n\n### 6. How are variables allocated?\n**Answer:** In the second pass, if an A-instruction `@symbol` is neither a decimal number, nor a predefined symbol, nor an existing label, it is treated as a variable. New variables are assigned free sequential RAM addresses starting at address **16** (`RAM[16]`, `RAM[17]`, `RAM[18]`, etc.).\n\n### 7. How does the assembler encode C-instructions?\n**Answer:** A C-instruction `dest=comp;jump` is encoded into 16 bits:\n- Bits 15\u201313: Fixed opcode `111`.\n- Bits 12\u20136: 1 bit for `a` and 6 bits for `c1..c6` (from `COMP_TABLE`).\n- Bits 5\u20133: 3 bits for `d1..d3` (from `DEST_TABLE`).\n- Bits 2\u20130: 3 bits for `j1..j3` (from `JUMP_TABLE`).\n\n### 8. What is my K-based additional feature?\n**Answer:** My personal roll number is `2501730041`, so $K = 41$ and $41 \\pmod 3 = 2$.\nThe feature is **Duplicate Label Detection**:\n- If a label declaration like `(LOOP)` appears more than once, the assembler prints a warning:\n  `Warning: Duplicate label 'LOOP' found at line 13. First definition is at line 6.`\n- The assembler retains the original address definition (avoiding silent overwrites) and continues safe assembly without crashing.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION C \u2014 SCREEN MEMORY & DISPLAY\n\n### 1. What is SCREEN?\n**Answer:** `SCREEN` is a predefined Hack symbol with value **16384** (`0x4000`). It represents the starting address of the memory-mapped display buffer in RAM.\n\n### 2. Why does screen memory start at 16384?\n**Answer:** Because the lower 16K words (`0` to `16383`) are allocated for general-purpose Data RAM (`RAM16K`). Address 16384 is the first address where bit 14 is `1` (`0100 0000 0000 0000`), cleanly separating I/O devices from data memory.\n\n### 3. Why are there 32 words in one screen row?\n**Answer:** The Hack screen resolution is **512 pixels wide \u00d7 256 pixels high**. Each memory word holds 16 bits, representing 16 horizontal pixels. Therefore, one screen row requires:\n$$\\frac{512 \\text{ pixels}}{16 \\text{ pixels/word}} = 32 \\text{ words per row}$$\n\n### 4. Why does each word represent 16 pixels?\n**Answer:** The Hack computer has a 16-bit word length. In the screen memory map, each bit directly maps to one pixel on the screen: bit 0 is the leftmost pixel of that 16-pixel chunk, and bit 15 is the rightmost.\n\n### 5. What does `-1` do?\n**Answer:** In 16-bit two's complement arithmetic, `-1` has the binary representation `1111 1111 1111 1111` (`0xFFFF`). Writing `-1` turns all 16 pixels in that memory word completely **black**.\n\n### 6. What does `0` do?\n**Answer:** `0` has the binary representation `0000 0000 0000 0000`. Writing `0` turns all 16 pixels in that memory word completely **white** (clearing/erasing the pixels).\n\n### 7. How is the box starting address calculated?\n**Answer:** For roll number `2501730041`, $K = 41$. The box starts at row 41 from the extreme left edge (offset 0):\n$$\\text{Starting Address} = \\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times K) = 16384 + (32 \\times 41) = 16384 + 1312 = \\mathbf{17696}$$\n\n---\n\n## SECTION D \u2014 BOX PROGRAM (`Box.asm`)\n\n### 1. How does keyboard checking work?\n**Answer:** The program reads memory location `KBD` (`RAM[24576]`). If no key is pressed, hardware sets `RAM[KBD] = 0`. If any key is pressed, it contains that key's non-zero ASCII code. The code executes:\n```assembly\n@KBD\nD=M\n@KEY_PRESSED\nD;JNE\n```\nIf `D != 0`, it branches to set `color = -1`; if `D == 0`, it sets `color = 0`.\n\n### 2. How is the box drawn?\n**Answer:** \n1. `color` is set to `-1` (`0xFFFF`).\n2. `row_addr` is initialized to `17696` (Row 41).\n3. `rows_left` is initialized to `20`.\n4. In `ROW_LOOP`, it writes `color` to `RAM[row_addr]` (Word 0: cols 0..15) and `RAM[row_addr + 1]` (Word 1: cols 16..31).\n5. It advances `row_addr = row_addr + 32` and decrements `rows_left = rows_left - 1`.\n6. Repeats until all 20 rows are filled with 32 black pixels.\n\n### 3. How is the box erased?\n**Answer:** Exactly the same loop runs, but `color` is set to `0`. Writing `0` to words at `row_addr` and `row_addr + 1` across all 20 rows restores the pixels back to white.\n\n### 4. How are the loops implemented?\n**Answer:** \n- `MAIN_LOOP`: An infinite polling loop that continuously reads `KBD`, sets the color, calls the box draw/erase routine, and jumps back to `MAIN_LOOP`.\n- `ROW_LOOP`: A counted loop using variable `rows_left`. When `rows_left <= 0` (`D;JLE`), the loop terminates and branches back to `MAIN_LOOP`.\n\n### 5. On-the-spot viva modification: How can the box be moved down?\n**Answer:** To move the box down, change the starting row address in `Box.asm`.\n- For example, to move the box down by 10 rows (from row 41 to row 51):\n  $$\\text{New Address} = \\text{SCREEN} + (32 \\times 51) = 16384 + 1632 = \\mathbf{18016}$$\n- In code, change:\n  ```assembly\n  @17696      // Change this\n  D=A\n  ```\n  to:\n  ```assembly\n  @18016      // New starting row address\n  D=A\n  ```\n\n### 6. On-the-spot viva modification: How can the box width be changed?\n**Answer:** \n- To make the box 48 pixels wide instead of 32 (3 words instead of 2):\n  Add a write to Word 2 inside `ROW_LOOP`:\n  ```assembly\n  @row_addr\n  A=M+1\n  A=A+1       // row_addr + 2\n  M=D         // Word 2 (cols 32..47)\n  ```\n- Or write a nested loop that iterates a `words_left` counter for each row.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION E \u2014 10 LIKELY VIVA QUESTIONS & CONFIDENT ANSWERS\n\n#### Q1: \"Show me where K is used in your HDL.\"\n**Answer:** \"Sir/Ma'am, $K$ is a software and application parameter, not a hardware chip parameter. The Hack hardware platform (`CPU.hdl`, `Memory.hdl`, `Computer.hdl`) is general-purpose. $K$ is used in my Python assembler feature ($K \\bmod 3 = 2$ for duplicate label detection) and in `Box.asm` where $K = 41$ sets the starting row and address (`17696`).\"\n\n#### Q2: \"Why did you use `RAM16K` instead of building custom RAM from individual bits?\"\n**Answer:** \"The Hack architecture builds hierarchical memory (`Bit` $\\rightarrow$ `Register` $\\rightarrow$ `RAM8` $\\rightarrow$ `RAM64` $\\rightarrow$ `RAM512` $\\rightarrow$ `RAM4K` $\\rightarrow$ `RAM16K`). In Project 5, `RAM16K` is loaded via the official built-in chip so the hardware simulator can run fast simulations without simulating 262,144 individual flip-flop gates.\"\n\n#### Q3: \"What happens if someone presses two keys at once on the Hack keyboard?\"\n**Answer:** \"The Hack keyboard hardware register only holds the scan code of a single pressed key at address 24576. In our program, we only test whether `RAM[KBD] != 0`. Any key press produces a non-zero value, so pressing any key or multiple keys draws the box.\"\n\n#### Q4: \"Why did your assembler fail when you originally wrote `M=M+D`?\"\n**Answer:** \"Because the Hack ALU has asymmetric inputs: the $x$ input is hardwired to the D-register and the $y$ input comes from A or M. The Hack machine instruction set defines the computation mnemonic as `D+M` ($x+y$), not `M+D`. The assembler caught this as an invalid mnemonic, which I diagnosed and corrected to `M=D+M`.\"\n\n#### Q5: \"How does your assembler detect duplicate labels?\"\n**Answer:** \"In `first_pass()`, I maintain a dictionary `label_decl_lines` that maps each label to the source line number where it was first declared. If a label is seen again, I issue a warning displaying both line numbers (`Warning: Duplicate label '...' at line X. First definition is at line Y.`), and I preserve the first address so the symbol table isn't corrupted.\"\n\n#### Q6: \"Why does the box start from column 0?\"\n**Answer:** \"The specification defines the box starting from the extreme left edge of the screen. Since row 41 begins at address 17696, writing into word 0 (17696) and word 1 (17697) draws the box precisely in pixels 0 to 31, aligning with the left border.\"\n\n#### Q7: \"What is the difference between `@17696` and `@row_addr`?\"\n**Answer:** \"`@17696` is a constant address pointing directly to screen memory. `@row_addr` is a variable allocated in data RAM at address 17. The program stores the value `17696` inside `RAM[row_addr]`, and uses pointer dereferencing (`A=M`) to access the screen word.\"\n\n#### Q8: \"How does the computer know whether an address refers to RAM or Screen?\"\n**Answer:** \"Through address decoding in `Memory.hdl`. Address bit 14 acts as the selector: if `address[14] == 0`, it accesses RAM (addresses 0..16383). If `address[14] == 1` and `address[13] == 0`, it accesses the Screen (addresses 16384..24575).\"\n\n#### Q9: \"Why does your assembler allocate variables starting at address 16?\"\n**Answer:** \"Because RAM addresses 0 through 15 are reserved for predefined virtual registers: `R0` through `R15`, which also alias `SP` (0), `LCL` (1), `ARG` (2), `THIS` (3), and `THAT` (4). User variables must begin at `RAM[16]` according to the Hack specification.\"\n\n#### Q10: \"If you want to make the box taller, say 40 rows instead of 20, what single line would you change?\"\n**Answer:** \"In `Box.asm`, change `@20` under `(DRAW_BOX)` to `@40` (`@40; D=A; @rows_left; M=D`). The row loop will then run for 40 consecutive rows.\"\n";



